Subject: SOP Semantic Programming Identity

& [SpecificationIdentity] is the canonical SJS identity clarification
  + [specification_uuid] is ce68d86a-ef59-4442-b87b-81e64a65af4e
  + [status] is language_identity_authority
  + [purpose] is establish SOP as plain articulated semantic programming rather than flexible descriptive prose

& [SOPIdentity] is the semantic equivalent of programming
  + [program_unit] is an articulated term, clause, declaration, relation, rule, constraint, contract, operation, or composition
  + [meaning] is the inspectable interpretation contract for a program unit
  + [execution] is meaning applied to state, input, attention, inference, authority, effects, and termination
  + [composition] is larger meaning formed from explicitly related program units
  = must: seek semantic coverage comparable to programming across data, types, control, functions, effects, modules, contracts, faults, concurrency, persistence, and interfaces
  = must: make intended meaning inspectable in plain articulated form
  = must: distinguish intended meaning from one model's transient interpretation

& [ArticulatedMeaningContract] is the minimum semantic addressability of every unit
  + [identity] is what the unit is
  + [intension] is the criteria and purpose that constitute its intended meaning
  + [extension] is the values or situations to which it applies when knowable
  + [context] is the scope, perspective, assumptions, and world in which it is interpreted
  + [relations] is how it composes, specializes, opposes, depends upon, or transforms other units
  + [inference] is what conclusions the unit licenses
  + [constraint] is what forms or states the unit permits, requires, or forbids
  + [operation] is what state transition the unit performs when executable
  + [authority] is who or what may assert, interpret, validate, or execute it
  + [evidence] is support, provenance, and justification
  + [faults] is ambiguity, contradiction, non-satisfaction, unsupported interpretation, and failure behavior
  + [termination] is completion, yield, suspension, or bounded failure
  = must: permit a field to be explicitly absent, unknown, inapplicable, deferred, disputed, or context-dependent

& [SemanticProgrammingDimensions] is the language completeness program
  + [declarative_semantics] is what entities, terms, relations, and truths mean
  + [operational_semantics] is how programs transform observable state
  + [denotational_semantics] is the stable meaning to which equivalent surface forms refer
  + [inference_semantics] is which conclusions follow and under what regime
  + [constraint_semantics] is which structures and states satisfy a contract
  + [effect_semantics] is which external or persistent changes are authorized
  + [interaction_semantics] is how agents, faculties, tools, and programs exchange and reconcile meaning
  + [temporal_semantics] is ordering, duration, version, reentry, and change
  + [failure_semantics] is how invalid, uncertain, contradictory, or incomplete states remain inspectable
  = must: define each dimension explicitly rather than rely on unrestricted natural-language intuition

& [PlainMeaningBoundary] is protection against false completeness
  + [plain] is articulable and inspectable at the user's chosen level of expansion
  + [precise] is discriminable enough to produce stable interpretation, validation, or execution
  + [contextual] is explicitly scoped rather than falsely universal
  + [recursive_expansion] is the ability to open a term into its defining clauses and referenced contracts
  = must: allow concise terms to address verbose semantic definitions without repeating them at every use
  = must: expose unresolved ambiguity rather than manufacture exhaustive definitions
  = must: terminate recursive definition through primitives, grounded references, observation, or an explicit unresolved boundary

& [OntologyRelationship] is the corrected standards boundary
  + [recognized_ontology] is formalized prior art, conformance material, reusable vocabulary, and an external interoperability contract
  + [SOP] is the intended general semantic programming system capable of natively articulating equivalent meanings
  + [ontology_import] is compilation of foreign semantic constructs into SOP declarations under a fidelity-preserving mapping
  = must: treat ontology standards as semantic test suites and libraries, not as a superior meaning layer
  = must: reproduce an imported construct's formal behavior before claiming semantic equivalence
  = must: preserve foreign semantics when SOP has not yet defined a native equivalent

& [LanguageKernelConsequence] is the work implied by this identity
  + [semantic_IR] is the normalized meaning representation shared by verbose, condensed, and machine forms
  + [definition_environment] is the scoped binding of identities, meanings, assumptions, and imported libraries
  + [type_and_relation_system] is the declared rules for membership, compatibility, hierarchy, and composition
  + [contract_system] is preconditions, postconditions, invariants, permissions, obligations, and faults
  + [inference_regime] is explicit consequence rules and their world assumptions
  + [execution_model] is state transition, effects, scheduling, concurrency, and termination
  + [reflection] is the ability for SOP to inspect and articulate its own program meanings
  = consequence: ontology review is part of discovering completeness requirements for the SOP semantic kernel

& [RequirementSet] is the traceable identity requirement collection
  + [SPI-001] is establish SOP as general semantic programming rather than descriptive markup
  + [SPI-002] is give every term, clause, and contract an inspectable meaning contract
  + [SPI-003] is support declarative, operational, denotational, inference, constraint, effect, interaction, temporal, and failure semantics
  + [SPI-004] is compile verbose, condensed, and machine forms to one semantic IR
  + [SPI-005] is use recognized ontologies as conformance suites, reusable libraries, and interoperability mappings
  + [SPI-006] is require behavioral equivalence before claiming lossless ontology conversion
  + [SPI-007] is expose contextual, unresolved, or disputed meaning without fabricating closure

& [SJSProcessingTrace] is the semantic programming identity trace
  + [fold_1_subject] is SOP as plain articulated programming of meaning
  + [fold_2_boundary] is inspectable intended semantics inside; transient model interpretation and false exhaustiveness outside
  + [fold_3_data_design] is identity, intension, extension, context, relations, inference, constraint, operation, authority, evidence, faults, and termination
  + [fold_4_governance] is standards provide conformance evidence while signed SOP definitions provide native authority
  + [fold_5_solution_shape] is semantic kernel, normalized IR, definition environment, contracts, inference regimes, execution, and reflection
  + [fold_6_fault_review] is vague prose, infinite definitional regress, context collapse, model-dependent interpretation, hidden effects, and false equivalence
  + [fold_7_phase_lock] is language identity clarified; kernel completeness and conformance proofs remain open

